notes.text:
Greek is fusional, like German, Arabic and Hebrew: one ending says several things at once.
→ in του λόγου the -ου is genitive and singular together, and another noun class uses another ending for the same job (της γυναίκας)

**Learning each noun with its gender and its table is a habit you bring:**
- German needs exactly that;
- Arabic and Hebrew add a root woven into vowel patterns;
- Chinese has no endings at all.

If your language is Semitic, Arabic and Hebrew work like it:
- a root of consonants woven into vowel patterns;
- ‘and’, prepositions and pronouns attached;
- many roots shared (k-t-b / כ-ת-ב ‘write’).

The work is in German, with three genders and case on the article, and in Chinese, with no endings at all.
